RUNNING KLIND UNDER SYSTEMD
---------------------------

contrib/systemd/klind.service is a hardened unit for klind. Copy it to
/etc/systemd/system/, set the ExecStart paths, then

    systemctl daemon-reload
    systemctl enable --now klind

ROOT, THEN NOT

klind starts as root so that it can bind ports below 1024, read a TLS key
that only root can read, and chroot. Once the sockets are open it chroots
(if 'chroot' is set) and drops to the 'uid' and 'gid' in klind.conf; the
TLS key manager (doc/KEYMGR) drops to them too. Give it an unprivileged
account, e.g.

    uid 65534
    gid 65534

klind refuses to start if it would keep root credentials: started as root
it needs both 'uid' and 'gid' (a uid alone would leave group root, and a
chroot alone is not enough, since root can leave a chroot), or an explicit

    allow_root yes

which is meant for tests and containers that are already unprivileged.

WHAT THE UNIT RESTRICTS

    CapabilityBoundingSet  only CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, CAP_SETUID, CAP_SETGID
                           and CAP_SYS_CHROOT; all are gone once klind drops
    NoNewPrivileges        no set-uid program can give privileges back
    ProtectSystem=strict   the file system is read-only, except
                           /run/klin, /var/lib/klin, /var/log/klin
                           (RuntimeDirectory, StateDirectory, LogsDirectory);
                           add ReadWritePaths= for anything else the site writes
    ProtectHome, PrivateTmp, PrivateDevices, DevicePolicy=closed
    ProtectKernel*, ProtectControlGroups, ProtectClock, ProtectHostname,
    ProtectProc=invisible, ProcSubset=pid
    RestrictNamespaces, RestrictRealtime, RestrictSUIDSGID, LockPersonality,
    MemoryDenyWriteExecute, KeyringMode=private, RemoveIPC
    RestrictAddressFamilies   AF_UNIX (the key manager), AF_INET, AF_INET6
    SystemCallFilter       @system-service without @privileged, @mount,
                           @debug etc., then @setuid and chroot back for the
                           drop; klind adds its own, narrower filter in each
                           worker (doc/SANDBOX)
    LimitNOFILE, TasksMax

'systemd-analyze security klind' rates the unit about 2.4 ("OK"). What is
left is the price of starting as root (User=, CAP_SET*, CAP_SYS_CHROOT,
@privileged) and of being a network server (PrivateNetwork=,
AF_INET/AF_INET6).

CGI scripts run inside the same unit: a script that needs to write
somewhere, or to JIT (MemoryDenyWriteExecute), needs a drop-in, e.g.
'systemctl edit klind':

    [Service]
    ReadWritePaths=/srv/www/uploads

WITHOUT ROOT AT ALL

If the key does not need protecting from klind's own user and chroot is not
used, systemd can start klind unprivileged instead (a drop-in):

    [Service]
    User=klin
    Group=klin
    AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
    CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE

and klind.conf then has no uid, gid or chroot.

TESTED

test/run_systemd_test.py (run as root; CI runs it with sudo) installs the
unit under /run, starts klind with it, and checks that it serves, that the
main process runs as 65534 with no capabilities, no_new_privs and a
seccomp filter, and that 'systemd-analyze security' stays at 3.0 or below.
The server suite checks the root refusal when run as root.
